Sorry for the slow summary : we are testing for a good solution since **begining 
of july** without success. Success come three days ago only. We configure 
networker (the server) on a *K200* with a *HP* FDDI card and it's working.

My original message :

vvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvvv
> 
> 
> From:	"Patrice.Legoux@gsi.fr" "Patrice LEGOUX"   10-SEP-1996 20:39:30.59
> To:	hpux-admin@cv.ruu.nl, alpha-osf-managers@ornl.gov, networker@iphase.com
> CC:	 
> Subj:	(Q): HP/FDDI/Networker configuration problem ?
> 
> Has anyone use successfully (or have been test with or without success) a 
> networker configuration like this :
> 
> o Server is :
> 
> 	- HP 9000/J210 - HP-UX B.10.01
> 	- Interphase FDDI (RJ45) card (512KB of cache)
> 	- MTI jukebox (4 DLT4000 drives)
> 
> o Clients are (or will be) :
> 
> 	- HP 800 and 900 series, V9 and V10
> 	- Dec Unix V3.2 and V4
> 
> o Network is based on DecConcentrator 900 MX, Dec GigaSwitch, etc (all network 
> equipments are from Digital)... Connections from server to clients are fully 
> FDDI based.
> 
> 
> Our problem is the server hang or crash when we try to backup more than 3 
> filesystems at the same time (on 3 drives). Then, when the 4th f.s backup 
> (using 
> the 4th drive) began, the FDDI card hang (ping doesn't work from and to the 
> host) or the system crashed (we have send the dump to hp for analysis and they 
> must come tomorow to get more datas for the hp network support). In our tests, 
> we only try Digital Unix clients.
> 
> We try to change MTU size from 4352 to 1500 without succes. All patches have 
> been set on systems.
> 
> We are thinking about an Interphase card buffer overflow or a server's EISA 
> capacity problem or maybe Alpha systems are too fast (?!?) or digital network 
> is 
> not 100% compatible with Interphase cards, but today, we don't make any tests 
> in 
> these ways. The only test we make was to change the Interphase card from a 128 
> MB cache to this 512KB. The difference was "netstat -i" dont return anymore 
> "Ierrs" with the 512KB card.
> 
> These are all informations I can send to the list today. You are welcome if 
> you 
> can give us any idea, information, comment...
> 
> 
> Patrice.
> 
> P.S : Sorry for 1) the crossposting between mailing lists (networker, 
> alpha-osf-managers and hpux-admin), 2) all faults in this text !!!

